[Music]  the marvelous missus Mazal recently  debuted its marvelous second season on  Amazon the show is known for its  rapid-fire quips and outstanding  ensemble casts but one thing you might  not actually pay attention to its  cinematography cinematographers  determine how a scene is shot and how  it's lit the second season of mrs. Mazal  starts with this long winding shot  through a switchboard room  we got a behind-the-scenes look at how  the Mazal team pulled off this dizzying  shot first of all we should talk about  long takes an early example comes from  Orson Welles Touch of Evil in 1958 the  film's opening shot goes uninterrupted  for about three minutes and 20 seconds  Alfonso Cuaron's children of men  involves multiple breathtaking one-shot  action scenes then in 2014 Alejandro  Gonzales and Yuri - made the entirety of  Birdman look like one long shot the one  from the marvelous mrs. Mazal comes at  the opening of season 2 the scene is set  at the fictitious department store where  midge Mazal played by Rachel Brosnahan  works I think in general we don't do a  lot of cutting on the show and there's a  lot of one-take scenes dialogue scenes  so it's part of our language for the  show in general that's em David Mullin  he's the show's director of photography  pulling off something like this  naturally involves a lot of planning  beforehand they discussed this one  single shot for about three or four  weeks before actually filming it Mullin  had to figure out what kind of camera to  use if he needed any special lenses and  if he was going to actually have to put  a camera inside a mail slot a CGI shot  of the piece of mail falling through the  slot helped them more easily capture the  shot while still giving a look of a  continuous take the next part was all  real the team waited for their cue to  enter trailing the mailman at the exact  moment he walked into the switchboard  room while they originally considered  using a more complicated cable system  they settled on Steadicam  this allowed them to tilt the camera  first from above the cart and then  behind the mailman into the room while  the camera remained relatively still  things got even tougher once they  actually enter the switchboard room  while cameras can move fast Mullen says  every shot is limited by the speed of  the human behind them plus they were  working in a tight space that had to fit  12 actors at least two crew members and  all the equipment and we turned into the  room and this was the part when we had  to decide whether to go on the outside  of the inside of this row they won with  the more difficult choice  inside the row the space was so tight  that the actresses had to move their  heads back so they wouldn't get hit in  the face at the same time the camera  couldn't capture that they were leaning  back so it was just a matter of timing  just as we're passing their nose  essentially they could start to lean out  of the way once there the camera ends up  focused fully on image except she  doesn't just sit still just to launch  across the room in a rolling chair to  take the call the camera crew had to  follow her there a space Mullen  approximates to be about five feet  across while circling around Mitch the  camera sometimes would have to end up in  a place where one of the women was  sitting in order to properly capture the  shot so if they wrapped around her that  actor would be blocking the shot that  meant the extras constantly had to  scramble around as the camera went  diagonally across the room so we would  always see them in the right position  this is somewhat visible in the final  scene but at times Brosnahan scene  partners had to physically push her  around so she ended up in the right spot  nailing this involved a lot of  rehearsing and teamwork between the  actors in the camera crew  there were some physical barriers such  as a file cabinet right here and a wall  right here it left them with very little  room to circle around as you can see  here the camera just makes it past all  their heads the shot ends once midge  finishes the call she turns around to  the group and the camera immediately  cuts to a reverse angle then the camera  goes back out the room and down the hall  for those of you at home timing in about  one minute and 17 seconds passes between  the letter making it down the mail slot  and mids turning to the group the shot  proved to be a perfect entry back into  the shows whimsical world the idea to  shoot the scene this way came from Mazal  creator Amy sherman-palladino who also  directed this episode a lot of these  super complicated shots are own or  imagination she's a formerly a dancer  and she loves movements with extras and  the camera that feel like a dance number  so she imagined the switchboard scene  which as you can imagine people sitting  in a console would not lend itself to  much movement but she wanted Mitch to  sort of slide quickly back and forth in  the rolling chair and the camera to keep  up with her and the camera to be able to  move among these women and these cables  and these these hand switching switches  while comedy is a genre that people  don't always think about when they  consider technical innovation there's no  way you could look away from the  dazzling visuals of the marvelous mrs.  nasal  [Music]  [Applause]

